![Photo by Ugi K. on Unsplash](cover.jpg)

In this article I will detail my adventures with the _Emacs_ editor.

## Why _Emacs_?

First. Why _Emacs_?. The truth is that I want to find a text editor
that I can use on any operating system. Ideally one that doesn't take up many
resources, since sometimes I like to use really old computers.

_VSCode_ is a great editor, however on the most humble computers
it can be very resource consuming.

_WebStorm_ is a great IDE, however, besides being demanding on resources,
it doesn't always have the latest versions of _Elixir_ in its syntax highlighter.
(It's not _WebStorm_'s fault).

_VIM_ I have used it for a while, although I never managed to learn beyond the basic
usage commands like _i_, _$_, _0_, _dd_, _yy_, _p_, _u_, _x_, and _:x_.

So my idea is to have two editors.

1. _Vim_ for quick edits (git commits, quick file editing in the terminal).
2. _Emacs_ to replace a _VSCode_ or _WebStorm_.

## Installation

The first thing is to install a modern version of _Emacs_. Preferably one above _25_.
From the official page of [GNU Emacs](https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/).

The installed version (as of today) is _29.3_.

After installing, the editor must be configured. Since I don't want to navigate those waters yet,
I have preferred to use a previously configured environment.

There are several, but the highlighted ones are two.

- [SpacEmacs](https://www.spacemacs.org/)
- [DoomEmacs](https://github.com/doomemacs/)

I chose _DoomEmacs_ simply because I like the game _Doom_.

To install _DoomEmacs_ you must run the following command.

```bash
git clone --depth 1 --single-branch https://github.com/doomemacs/doomemacs ~/.config/emacs
~/.config/emacs/bin/doom install
```

Note that the configuration will be saved in `~/.config/emacs`. Some tutorials and documentation
have an alternative path which is `~/.emacs.d`. Both directories will work, but only one
must be present, otherwise _Emacs_ will take the configuration from `~/.emacs.d`,
it is recommended to use `~/.config/emacs` since it doesn't pollute our _$HOME_ directory. If _DoomEmacs_
doesn't load, check that both directories don't exist, only one.
You can make sure by running the command `rm -rf ~/.emacs.d/`.

#### PATH

It is recommended to configure the _$PATH_ environment variable with the _doom_
binaries so that they can be executed freely.

```bash
PATH="$HOME/.config/emacs/bin":$PATH
```

#### Recommendations

A very useful command is `doom doctor` which allows detecting problems in our configuration
or if some essential program is missing.

We also recommend installing _Node.js_ for the compilation of _Markdown_ files (optional).

```bash
npm install -g stylelint
npm install -g marked
npm install -g js-beautify
```

Also the _editorconfig_ package

```bash
brew install editorconfig
```

And the ripgrep and grep packages

```bash
brew install ripgrep
brew install grep
```

Add the following to `.bashrc`

```bash
if [ -d "$(brew --prefix)/opt/grep/libexec/gnubin" ]; then
    PATH="$(brew --prefix)/opt/grep/libexec/gnubin:$PATH"
fi
```

- See: <https://asdf-vm.com/> to install _Node.js_.
- See: <https://brain.mikecordell.com/posts/20220709152320-doom_emacs/>

#### MacOS

On _MacOS_ it is recommended to install _Emacs_ from homebrew as follows:

##### Install Dependencies

```bash
brew install git ripgrep
brew install coreutils fd
xcode-select --install
```

##### Compile Emacs

```bash
brew tap railwaycat/emacsmacport
brew install emacs-mac --with-modules --with-native-compilation --with-starter --with-unlimited-select --with-emacs-big-sur-icon
ln -s /usr/local/opt/emacs-mac/Emacs.app /Applications/Emacs.app
```

For more details check the guide <https://github.com/doomemacs/doomemacs/blob/master/docs/getting_started.org#with-homebrew>

### Fonts

The installation of fonts is important, especially the [Nerd Fonts](https://www.nerdfonts.com/)
since they have special icons that will make everything prettier. My recommended font is [JetBrains Mono](https://github.com/ryanoasis/nerd-fonts/releases/download/v3.2.0/JetBrainsMono.zip).

_DoomEmacs_ uses a special font called _NFM.ttf_, which can be installed by following these steps:

1. Open _Emacs_ normally.
2. Press the keys `Alt + x` (M-x).
3. Find the command `nerd-icons-install-fonts` and run it.
4. Restart _Emacs_.


### Installation of Modes

_Emacs_ works with different modes. For example, if we have a file that contains _Markdown_ and _JSON_ in it,
we can switch between different modes to get syntax highlighting and special commands
only designed for that type of file.

We are going to install _markdown_ and _json_.

1. Press the _Space_ key and then _f_ to finally press the _P_ key (uppercase) (SPC f-P)
2. We look for the `init.el` file and open it for editing.
3. Near line 144 we can activate both _markdown_ and _json_.
4. Close _Emacs_.
5. Run the `doom sync` command to download the components (in our terminal).
6. Open _Emacs_.

![init.el](init.png)

For example, if we open a file we can switch between the modes and alternate between them to get
an appropriate syntax highlighting.

To switch a mode we only have to press `M-x` (_Alt_ and then the _x_ key) and type
`markdown` or `json` to activate the respective mode.

*Note*: If we want to exit the section to change modes we can press `C-g` (Ctrl + g).

_Markdown_ mode:

![Markdown Mode](markdown.png)

_JSON_ mode:

![JSON Mode](json.png)

#### Evil Mode

The _evil_ mode. It has to do with enabling the _VIM_
commands for the use of _Emacs_, which makes things much easier for
_Vim_ users like me.

In _DoomEmacs_ it comes activated by default.

Anyway, I would recommend learning the _Emacs_ commands to navigate the files
since they are also used on other platforms (Hello _IEX_ from Erlang and Elixir).

You may wonder why the crazy nomenclatures of _Emacs_.
The truth is simply because of its age. _Emacs_ existed long before the standards
like _CTRL C_ and _CTRL V_. So it had to invent its own.

The _Emacs_ nomenclature could be considered an alternative standard
to <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IBM_Common_User_Access> (CUA).


#### Personal Opinion

This modality has pleased me a lot, because other editors
tend to make syntax highlighting a bit difficult when there is a file
with multiple languages mixed in (Hello PHP).

I think it's great to be able to easily switch between languages and also have
special support commands.

